select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/654"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Light-Flyweight, Greco-Roman (≤48 kg), Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "30 July –  1 August 1984 — 12:00-15:00-18:00-20:30 (all days)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/633"} "Anaheim Convention Center, Anaheim, California, USA"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/129190"} "12 from 12 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Two groups of competitors.  Placement within each group decided by a series of matches with positive points awarded for each result, from 4.0 for victory by fall, to 0.0 for defeat by fall.  When three wrestlers remained within a group, they wrestled a round-robin series to determine the top three placements within the group.  The winners of each group then wrestled for 1st and 2nd place.  Runners-up in each group wrestled for 3rd and 4th place.  Third-place finishers within each group wrestled for 5th and 6th place."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "Turkey’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/60296"} "Salih Bora"] ", silver medalist at the 1981-82 World Championships, was favored, as all the other recent major international medalists could not compete because of the Soviet-led boycott. In the first match of Group A he met Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/58937"} "Vincenzo Maenza"] ", who had won a bronze medal at the 1984 European Championships. Maenza prevailed, 3-0, in points, which effectively eliminated Bora from gold medal contention. Maenza went to the final match where he met West German " [:a {:href "/athletes/58412"} "Markus Scherer"] ", easily defeating him, 12-0, to win the gold medal. Maenza was unscored on in Los Angeles, winning his four matches, 31-0. Bora placed fourth, defeated in the bronze medal match by Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/59081"} "Ikuzo Saito"] "."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Maenza would defend his gold medal at Seoul in 1988, and added a silver medal at Barcelona in 1992. He had a long career, winning four medals at the Europeans from 1981-87, and three at the Worlds from 1982-87, including a World title in 1985 and a European title in 1987."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Round Eliminated"] [:th {:data-toggle "tooltip", :data-placement "right", :data-original-title "Classification Points", :class "footnote"} "CP"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "124"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/58937"} "Vincenzo Maenza"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "90"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/58412"} "Markus Scherer"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRG.png"}] "FRG"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {:class "bib"} "145"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/59081"} "Ikuzo Saito"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {:class "bib"} "234"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/60296"} "Salih Bora"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/TUR"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle